	(lib_symbols)
	(text_box "RF TRACE OPTIMIZATION PROCEDURE\n(Between diplexer and SMA connector)\n\nPlacing an inductor in shunt (connected between the signal line and ground) and a capacitor in series (connected in-line along the signal line) forms a classic high-pass L-network or a portion of a π-network.\nHow These Elements Physically Transform the Impedance\n1. Shunt Inductor (Signal to Ground)\nAt DC / Low Frequencies: It acts as a short circuit to ground, which provides built-in ESD protection and drains static charges that build up on the antenna.\nAt High RF Frequencies (2.4 GHz / 5 GHz): It acts as an inductive reactance (+jX). On a Smith Chart, adding a shunt inductor shifts the antenna impedance clockwise along constant-admittance circles, canceling out unwanted parasitic capacitance from wider pads (like SMA landings) or nearby enclosure plastics.\n2. Series Capacitor (In-Line on Signal Trace)\nAt DC: It acts as a DC block, preventing any accidental DC voltage from reaching the RF diplexer or antenna.\nAt High RF Frequencies: It acts as a capacitive reactance (−jX). On a Smith Chart, adding a series capacitor shifts the impedance counter-clockwise along constant-resistance circles, canceling out unwanted series inductance from traces or component legs.\nThe Common Empirical Topologies for Wi-Fi Tuning\nWhen tuning by hand using trial-and-error (monitoring RSSI and throughput), you will typically test one of two 2-element configurations within your 3-pad π-network footprint:\nTopology A: Series Cap + Shunt Inductor (High-Pass Match)\nSeries Position: Small C0G capacitor (1.5 pF−3.3 pF)\nShunt Position (Antenna Side): Small high-Q inductor (1.2 nH−3.9 nH) to ground\nBest used when: The antenna/trace looks capacitive and low-impedance (common when an antenna is detuned by being close to a plastic case or ground pour).\nTopology B: Series Inductor + Shunt Cap (Low-Pass Match)\nSeries Position: Small high-Q inductor (1.0 nH−2.7 nH)\nShunt Position (Antenna Side): Small C0G capacitor (0.5 pF−1.5 pF) to ground\nBest used when: The antenna/trace looks inductive and high-impedance (common when the trace is slightly too long or thin).\nSummary Guidelines for Hand-Tuning\nStart Simple: Begin with a 0 Ω series resistor and unpopulated shunts (DNP). If your RSSI is strong and throughput is stable, leave it as-is.\nMake Incremental Swaps: Change only one component at a time (e.g., test a 2.2 pF series cap first before adding a shunt element) and re-measure average RSSI across at least 100 packets.\nUse High-Quality Components: Ensure any test inductors are high-Q wirewound or ceramic RF inductors (like Murata LQG or LQP series) and capacitors are C0G/NP0 dielectrics."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 471.17 27.94 0)
