	(text_box "RF TRACE OPTIMIZATION\n(Between diplexer and SMA connector)\n\nOptimizing an RF trace without a Vector Network Analyzer (VNA) or Spectrum Analyzer requires relying on relative metric optimization using the ESP32-C5's built-in radio diagnostics. Since you cannot measure complex impedance (S \n11\n​	\n ) directly, you will optimize for maximum energy transfer by measuring the chip's Received Signal Strength Indicator (RSSI) and throughput/packet-loss metrics across different physical hardware configurations.\nStrategy: Empirical RSSI & Throughput Optimization\n1. Establish a Controlled RF Test Environment\nFixed Distance & Orientation: Set up a reference Wi-Fi router or access point (AP) at a fixed distance (e.g., 5 to 10 meters away through 1-2 walls to avoid receiver saturation).\nFix the Spatial Plane: Mount both the AP and your custom ESP32-C5 PCB on non-conductive surfaces (like wooden or plastic tables). Do not touch the board during measurements, as body capacitance will detune the 5 GHz antenna.\nTest Both Bands: Perform testing separately on a dedicated 2.4 GHz channel (e.g., Channel 6) and a 5 GHz Wi-Fi 6 channel (e.g., Channel 36 or 149).\n2. Run Built-In Diagnostic Firmware\nUse Espressif's Wi-Fi diagnostic APIs (esp_wifi_get_ap_info() or iPerf performance test firmware) to log key metrics:\nAverage RSSI (dBm): Sample RSSI continuously over 100+ packets to filter out multipath fading noise.\nPacket Error Rate (PER) / Retry Rate: Higher reflection on the RF trace causes phase distortion (EVM degradation), raising packet retransmission counts under heavy load.\nTX/RX Throughput (iPerf3): Run a TCP/UDP throughput test.\nStep-by-Step Trial-and-Error Tuning Procedure\nSince your series position on the π-network starts with a 0 Ω jumper and empty shunts (DNP), you can iterate through a small matrix of low-cost high-Q RF passive components (0402 C0G caps and high-Q inductors):\n1\nRecord the Baseline (Direct 0-Ohm Connection)\nEstablishes performance floor\nBoot the board with your default 0 Ω series bridge and DNP shunts. Measure and record average RSSI, packet retry rate, and iPerf throughput on both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz.\nVerification: Confirm stable baseline readings across 3 distinct test runs without moving the board.\n\n2\nTest Series Capacitance Tweak\nAddresses SMA pad parasitic capacitance\nSwap the 0 Ω resistor for a 10 pF C0G capacitor (acts as an RF pass-through while blocking any DC offset) or a 1.8 pF−2.7 pF capacitor.\nVerification: If RSSI improves by ≥2 dBm on 5 GHz, the original trace/pad transition was overly inductive and needed small capacitive compensation.\n\n3\nTest Shunt Inductor / Capacitor Trial\nCompensates for antenna detuning\nIf the baseline RSSI is poor or drops significantly when placed inside a plastic enclosure, try placing a small 1.0 nH−2.7 nH high-Q inductor or 0.5 pF−1.2 pF capacitor in the shunt position closest to the antenna.\nVerification: Compare average RSSI against the baseline; retain the component that yields the highest dBm reading (closer to 0 dBm).\n\nSummary Tuning Matrix\nMetric Shift Observed	Likely Underlying Issue	Compensating Action\n5 GHz RSSI is 5+ dB worse than 2.4 GHz	SMA pad capacitance or long trace inductance detuning 5 GHz band	Reduce series component value or add a small shunt inductor (1.2 nH−2.2 nH)\nRSSI is good, but iPerf throughput is low / PER high	High EVM / phase noise caused by trace reflection (S \n11\n​	\n )	Tweak the series element value (1.5 pF→3.3 pF) to sharpen LO phase matching\nSignal drops drastically when placed in enclosure	Plastic case dielectric detuning the antenna	Add a small shunt capacitor (0.5 pF−1.0 pF) to pull antenna resonance back down"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
