	)
	(text_box "RF TRACE OPTIMIZATION WO NETWORK ANALYZER (TTRIAL AND ERROR)\n(Between diplexer and SMA connector)\n\nEven without vector network analyzer (VNA) or spectrum analyzer equipment to measure S \n11\n​	\n  or harmonic performance, yes, it still makes complete sense to include the 3-component π-network footprint on your board.\nSince this is a non-commercial build, the justification shifts away from regulatory certification and toward flexibility, ease of bring-up, and simple troubleshooting.\nWhy the Footprint Is Still Worth It (No Equipment Required)\n1. Zero Cost & Zero Risk\nAdding three empty 0402 pads in-line with your RF trace costs nothing on your PCB fabrication order and takes up less than 1.5 mm×1.5 mm of board real estate.\nDefault state: Populate a 0 Ω resistor (or a solder bridge) across the series pad and leave the two shunt pads empty (DNP).\nIn this configuration, your circuit operates exactly as the direct trace you simulated in ADS, with zero added insertion loss or signal degradation.\n2. Emergency \"Trial and Error\" Fixing\nIf you assemble the board and discover Wi-Fi range or signal quality on 5 GHz is poor, having those physical pads gives you an easy way to experiment without cutting traces or scraping solder mask off your board:\nEnclosure / Hand Detuning: If placing the board inside a 3D-printed case or holding it in your hand degrades the signal, dropping a small high-Q capacitor (0.5 pF−2.2 pF) or inductor (1 nH−3.9 nH) into the shunt or series pads can often recover lost antenna performance through trial-and-error.\nDC Blocking / RF Attenuation: If you ever need to introduce a small series capacitance (e.g., 10 pF) for DC isolation or test different antenna types (like swapping between an SMA whip antenna and a ceramic chip antenna), the pads are already there.\n3. PCB Trace & Connector Tolerances\nEven in hobbyist or prototype PCB manufacturing, layer thickness, copper etching tolerances, and SMA solder joint geometry vary. A short trace that calculates as 50 Ω in ADS can land at 40 Ω−60 Ω in practice. Having the footprint gives you an immediate physical place to adjust the RF path if needed.\nRecommended Approach for Your PCB\n1\nPlace the 0402 pi-Network Footprint\nLayout preparation\nPlace a standard 3-component π-network footprint directly at the SMA connector launch pad on Layer 1.\nVerification: Ensure the ground pads for the two shunt positions connect directly to the Layer 2 ground plane with short, wide traces and ground vias.\n\n2\nPopulate with Default Jumper\nInitial assembly\nSolder a 0 Ω (0402) resistor in the series position and leave both shunt component pads unpopulated (DNP).\nVerification: Perform a simple continuity check between the diplexer common port pad and the SMA center pin using a multimeter to confirm a solid connection.\n\n3\nTest Wi-Fi Range and Throughput\nFunctional bring-up\nBoot the ESP32-C5 and evaluate RSSI (Received Signal Strength Indicator) and throughput across both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands.\nVerification: If connection strength and data rates match expected performance, leave the default 0 Ω bridge in place-no further tuning is necessary."
